      "results": [
        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-674",
          "ruleIndex": 0,
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "Raw MCP tool arguments are canonicalized with unbounded recursion and quadratic indentation — memory-exhaustion DoS by any token-holding client.\n\nThe untrusted source is `params.arguments` of every MCP `tools/call`, fetched raw (server.ex:55) and passed to Vorpruefung before any depth/size bound; the vendored anubis layer only requires arguments to be an open `:map` (anubis message.ex:58 \"arguments\" => :map; peri.ex:796 validates :map with a bare is_map check), so arbitrarily deep nested objects pass. On any schema violation the refusal PDR hashes the raw arguments with `JSON.canonical/1`, a non-tail-recursive encoder that recurses once per attacker-chosen nesting level and builds `pad(depth) = List.duplicate(\"  \", depth)` at every level (json.ex:598, 618), making both live heap and output binary Θ(depth²).\n\nImpact: Full availability loss of the mediation service and its PDR audit journal: a single authenticated request (~150 KB) drives tens of gigabytes of allocation in the anubis task process (Θ(depth²) cons cells plus a Θ(depth²)-byte flattened binary), crashing the mediator VM or exhausting machine memory; the attack is repeatable and parallelizable with a tiny payload (~5 bytes per nesting level, 8 MB body limit allows ~1.3M levels).\n\nExploit scenario: pi, the sandboxed agent that legitimately holds the bearer token and connects to the 0600 socket, POSTs a tools/call for any advertised tool (e.g. \"ping\") with arguments = {\"a\":{\"a\":{\"a\":… ~50,000 levels deep …}}}. The OTP json decoder is iterative (explicit stack, no depth cap) and Peri's :map leaves values unchecked, so the term reaches Vorpruefung.check; Params.check rejects it as an unknown field, and refused/5 then canonicalizes the raw nested map to bind the intent_hash — the quadratic encoder allocates ~8·depth² bytes of heap and never returns, taking the mediator and the machine's memory with it.\n\nPreconditions: Attacker is a local same-uid process that can reach the 0600 mediator unix socket and holds the bearer token (pi, the MCP client, holds it by design; any second token-holding session also qualifies); Mediator running with any room/registry.\n\nRecommendation: Bound the raw arguments before hashing them: in Vorpruefung.check, pre-walk the arguments and reject (or cheaply cap) nesting depth and total encoded size before Params.check and before building the refusal PDR; independently give KomkiPolicy.JSON.canonical/encode_value an explicit depth limit that fails with a structured error instead of recursing per input level. Optionally spawn the per-request anubis task with spawn_opt max_heap_size so a runaway request dies alone instead of starving the VM.\n\nCWE-674. Severity HIGH, confidence high."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "apps/komki_mediator/lib/komki_mediator/vorpruefung.ex",
                  "uriBaseId": "%SRCROOT%"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 117,
                  "snippet": {
                    "text": "\"intent_hash\" => Hash.sha256_hex(JSON.canonical(%{\"tool\" => tool, \"params\" => args})),"
                  }
                }
              },
              "logicalLocations": [
                {
                  "name": "KomkiMediator.Vorpruefung.refused/5",
                  "fullyQualifiedName": "KomkiMediator.Vorpruefung.refused/5"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "properties": {
            "claudeSecurityPlugin": {
              "id": "F1",
              "title": "Raw MCP tool arguments are canonicalized with unbounded recursion and quadratic indentation — memory-exhaustion DoS by any token-holding client",
