        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-863",
          "ruleIndex": 1,
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "Mach-O load commands define the sandbox's own read scopes: attacker-named dylib directories become file-read* allow rules with no allowlist, existence or breadth check.\n\nThe untrusted source is the scanned binary's Mach-O LC_LOAD_DYLIB/LC_RPATH strings (and those of every transitive dep file the walk visits); the dangerous sink is that these strings are emitted verbatim as `(allow file-read* (subpath dir))` grants in the deny-default profile — `Path.dirname/1` of any absolute declared path, e.g. a declared dep `/libfoo.dylib`, produces `(allow file-read* (subpath \"/\"))`, the whole filesystem.\n\nImpact: Complete confidentiality break of the sandbox boundary the component exists to enforce: the confined process gains file-read over any directory named in its own Mach-O, up to every file the user can read (SSH keys, vault notes, komki secrets). For komki the confined program is the inference server that also drives the pi agent's tool calls, so stolen bytes can be exfiltrated off-machine through the agent's web-search tool path (queries leave via the egress proxy), despite the profile forbidding the sandboxed process direct network egress.\n\nExploit scenario: A user installs an attacker-influenced llama-server build (or anything shadowing `llama-server` earlier in PATH — komki discovers it via find_executable, config.ex:230-232). `komki init` silently pins its bytes (steps.ex:157; pins verify byte stability, not origin) and generates the llama profile, whose dylib layer now contains `(allow file-read* (subpath \"/\"))` because the binary declares a root-level dependency such as `/libfoo.dylib` — the grant survives the byte-identity doctor check because regeneration reproduces it deterministically. The sandboxed inference server then reads SSH keys and confidential vault documents at will, and as the agent's local model it can carry them off-machine by embedding them in web_search queries that pi routes out through the egress proxy. The same widening applies to the mediator/connector/egress profiles via the crypto-NIF walk when any visited, unpinned dylib (e.g. under the user-writable Homebrew prefix) is modified before the next `komki setup`.\n\nPreconditions: Attacker influences the Mach-O load commands of the scanned binary (llama-server supply-chain build, PATH-shadowed binary) or of a transitive dep file the otool walk visits (same-user write to an unpinned dylib, e.g. /opt/homebrew); komki (re)generates the profile while the attacker-controlled Mach-O is in place (komki init, or setup/refresh when paths change); The program pins gate does not help: init pins whatever bytes are present and never asks about origin.\n\nRecommendation: Bound and validate the granted scopes at the MachODeps seam: reject dep directories outside a caller-declared allowlist of roots (install prefix, Homebrew prefix), require the dep to resolve to an existing file before granting its directory, and reject breadth ceilings (dirname \"/\", minimum path depth). Additionally, record the load-command-derived scope set (or hashes of visited dep files) alongside the program pins so that scope drift fails closed the way byte drift already does.\n\nCWE-863. Severity HIGH, confidence medium."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "apps/seatbelt/lib/seatbelt/macho_deps.ex",
                  "uriBaseId": "%SRCROOT%"
                },
