        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-287",
          "ruleIndex": 2,
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "komki-tresor releases the ECDH shared secret to any caller, with caller-chosen text as the only thing the approving person sees.\n\nThe shared secret that decrypts every ktv2 seal is printed on stdout to whichever same-user process spawns komki-tresor; the sole authorization signal in the Touch ID dialog is the `reason` argv string supplied by that same unauthenticated caller (the helper's own header says \"the person at the Mac sees the reason\"), so a same-user process can request the key agreement for a deployment's existing key under a reason byte-identical to komki's own routine prompt. The data flow leads out of the sealed_envelope component here: SealedEnvelope.open/2 -> agree -> this helper.\n\nImpact: Complete confidentiality loss of every secret the seal exists to protect: the komki keychain credentials (Brave API key, ratsprojekte-hub token), the ratsprojekte SQLCipher database key, and the 'ris-kassetten' cassette master key. The Touch ID barrier the design claims (\"Oeffnen geht nur ... mit Touch ID\", tresor.ex:9-12) reduces to one tap on a prompt the attacker fully controls the text of.\n\nExploit scenario: Malware running as the user — the exact adversary the seal is designed against — reads the at-rest envelope (schluessel.ktv2 is a plain same-user-readable file; keychain items created via /usr/bin/security are readable by any process invoking the same binary), extracts `ref` and `eph`, and runs: echo \"<b64 ref> <b64 eph>\" | komki-tresor agree --reason \"ratsprojekte entsperren\". The person sees the identical prompt shown at every legitimate unlock, taps Touch ID, and the malware receives the shared secret on stdout, derives HKDF-SHA256(shared, eph, \"ktv2\"), and AES-256-GCM-decrypts the box: it now holds the SQLCipher key (or any other sealed secret, one indistinguishable prompt each).\n\nPreconditions: Attacker code running as the same local user (no extra privileges); The person is at the Mac and approves the Touch ID prompt, which is byte-identical to komki's own routine prompt; komki-tresor binary discoverable (PATH / beside komki / priv dir).\n\nRecommendation: Do not hand the raw shared secret to an unauthenticated caller: bind the enclave key to a keychain access group or store the wrapped key as a keychain item whose ACL names komki, so the macOS access prompt identifies the requesting application; render a verifiable key fingerprint in the prompt instead of relying on caller-supplied free text; at minimum document that `--reason` is untrusted caller text and that the reason is the entire consent channel.\n\nCWE-287. Severity HIGH, confidence high."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "apps/komki_cli/native/komki-tresor/main.swift",
                  "uriBaseId": "%SRCROOT%"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 56,
                  "snippet": {
                    "text": "  context.localizedReason = reason"
                  }
                }
              },
              "logicalLocations": [
                {
                  "name": "agree(_ line: String, reason: String) -> String",
                  "fullyQualifiedName": "agree(_ line: String, reason: String) -> String"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
