              "exploit_scenario": "Malware running as the user — the exact adversary the seal is designed against — reads the at-rest envelope (schluessel.ktv2 is a plain same-user-readable file; keychain items created via /usr/bin/security are readable by any process invoking the same binary), extracts `ref` and `eph`, and runs: echo \"<b64 ref> <b64 eph>\" | komki-tresor agree --reason \"ratsprojekte entsperren\". The person sees the identical prompt shown at every legitimate unlock, taps Touch ID, and the malware receives the shared secret on stdout, derives HKDF-SHA256(shared, eph, \"ktv2\"), and AES-256-GCM-decrypts the box: it now holds the SQLCipher key (or any other sealed secret, one indistinguishable prompt each).",
