        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-732",
          "ruleIndex": 4,
          "level": "warning",
          "message": {
            "text": "komki_mock persists adapter state (Replay cassette bodies, accepted Brave token) with default world-readable file permissions.\n\nThe Replay adapter ingests real recorded RIS exchanges that ris_recorder.exs deliberately keeps owner-only (File.chmod!(file, 0o600) at its line 302, dir 0o700 at line 377, because cassettes hold real non-public data), and KomkiMock.Server.persist/write_json re-persists the full cassette bodies into state.json via File.write! with default 0644 permissions inside a File.mkdir_p!'d 0755 dir — komki_mock never chmods, unlike every other secret-writing component in the repo.\n\nImpact: Confidentiality breach on the local host: real non-public municipal data from recorded authenticated RIS endpoints (protected meeting parts, personnel matters, real-estate deals) and the accepted Brave mock token become readable by every other local OS user (loopback listeners and /tmp are per-host, not per-user; 0644 file in a 0755 dir). It also republishes any credential that survived the recorder's scrub lists, which cover only named headers (authorization/cookie/set-cookie), the /auth path and exact body keys (login/password/token/access_token/jwt) — e.g. a session token in a query string or under another field name is recorded verbatim and lands in state.json. This defeats the recorder's explicit design goal (\"Verzeichnis und Dateien sind nur fuer den Eigentuemer lesbar\"). requests.jsonl (File.write! ... [:append], server.ex:199) and ports.json share the same permissive-default weakness; the unauthenticated control port (GET /state, control_plug.ex:32, bound only to 127.0.0.1 at server.ex:265) exposes the same material to any local process.\n\nExploit scenario: A developer records a real RIS session with ris_recorder.exs (cassettes land owner-only under ~/.local/share/kommunalpolitik_ki/ris-cassettes/<ts>) and replays it per the documented workflow `mix komki_mock.serve --adapter KomkiMock.Adapters.Replay --seed <out> --dir /tmp/mock-ris` (ris_recorder.exs header, komki_cli README:218). At boot, load_or_seed (server.ex:204-223) persists every recorded response body into /tmp/mock-ris/state.json with 0644 inside a 0755 directory. A second local user on the shared dev machine reads /tmp/mock-ris/state.json and obtains the municipality's non-public meeting content the recorder had locked to the owner.\n\nPreconditions: Operator replays real recorded cassettes through the Replay adapter (the recorder's documented playback path) or runs the Brave mock with a key set via PUT /data / a seed; Default umask (e.g. 022) so File.write!/File.mkdir_p! yield 0644/0755 — komki_mock itself never chmods (verified: no chmod in lib or tests); A reader beyond the owner exists: another local OS user, or any tool/agent working in the repo when --dir is chosen inside it (serve.ex:50 expands the path without the recorder's repo check).\n\nRecommendation: Persist state with owner-only permissions, following the repo's own convention (ris_recorder.exs:302/377, sidecar token, komki_cli sockets, mediator): after File.write! apply File.chmod!(path, 0o600) (or open with restrictive modes) for state.json, requests.jsonl, ports.json, and File.chmod!(dir, 0o700) for the state dir; additionally warn or refuse when the Replay adapter would copy cassettes into a world-readable or repo-internal --dir, mirroring the recorder's check_out.\n\nCWE-732. Severity MEDIUM, confidence high."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "apps/komki_mock/lib/komki_mock/server.ex",
                  "uriBaseId": "%SRCROOT%"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 254,
                  "snippet": {
                    "text": "    File.write!(tmp, JSON.encode!(value))"
                  }
                }
              },
              "logicalLocations": [
                {
                  "name": "KomkiMock.Server.write_json/2 (via persist/1)",
                  "fullyQualifiedName": "KomkiMock.Server.write_json/2 (via persist/1)"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "properties": {
            "claudeSecurityPlugin": {
              "id": "F9",
              "title": "komki_mock persists adapter state (Replay cassette bodies, accepted Brave token) with default world-readable file permissions",
              "impact": "Confidentiality breach on the local host: real non-public municipal data from recorded authenticated RIS endpoints (protected meeting parts, personnel matters, real-estate deals) and the accepted Brave mock token become readable by every other local OS user (loopback listeners and /tmp are per-host, not per-user; 0644 file in a 0755 dir). It also republishes any credential that survived the recorder's scrub lists, which cover only named headers (authorization/cookie/set-cookie), the /auth path and exact body keys (login/password/token/access_token/jwt) — e.g. a session token in a query string or under another field name is recorded verbatim and lands in state.json. This defeats the recorder's explicit design goal (\"Verzeichnis und Dateien sind nur fuer den Eigentuemer lesbar\"). requests.jsonl (File.write! ... [:append], server.ex:199) and ports.json share the same permissive-default weakness; the unauthenticated control port (GET /state, control_plug.ex:32, bound only to 127.0.0.1 at server.ex:265) exposes the same material to any local process.",
              "file": "apps/komki_mock/lib/komki_mock/server.ex",
              "line": 254,
              "description": "The Replay adapter ingests real recorded RIS exchanges that ris_recorder.exs deliberately keeps owner-only (File.chmod!(file, 0o600) at its line 302, dir 0o700 at line 377, because cassettes hold real non-public data), and KomkiMock.Server.persist/write_json re-persists the full cassette bodies into state.json via File.write! with default 0644 permissions inside a File.mkdir_p!'d 0755 dir — komki_mock never chmods, unlike every other secret-writing component in the repo.",
              "exploit_scenario": "A developer records a real RIS session with ris_recorder.exs (cassettes land owner-only under ~/.local/share/kommunalpolitik_ki/ris-cassettes/<ts>) and replays it per the documented workflow `mix komki_mock.serve --adapter KomkiMock.Adapters.Replay --seed <out> --dir /tmp/mock-ris` (ris_recorder.exs header, komki_cli README:218). At boot, load_or_seed (server.ex:204-223) persists every recorded response body into /tmp/mock-ris/state.json with 0644 inside a 0755 directory. A second local user on the shared dev machine reads /tmp/mock-ris/state.json and obtains the municipality's non-public meeting content the recorder had locked to the owner.",
              "preconditions": [
                "Operator replays real recorded cassettes through the Replay adapter (the recorder's documented playback path) or runs the Brave mock with a key set via PUT /data / a seed",
                "Default umask (e.g. 022) so File.write!/File.mkdir_p! yield 0644/0755 — komki_mock itself never chmods (verified: no chmod in lib or tests)",
                "A reader beyond the owner exists: another local OS user, or any tool/agent working in the repo when --dir is chosen inside it (serve.ex:50 expands the path without the recorder's repo check)"
              ],
              "category": "Incorrect Permission Assignment for Critical Resource",
              "severity": "MEDIUM",
              "confidence": "high",
              "recommendation": "Persist state with owner-only permissions, following the repo's own convention (ris_recorder.exs:302/377, sidecar token, komki_cli sockets, mediator): after File.write! apply File.chmod!(path, 0o600) (or open with restrictive modes) for state.json, requests.jsonl, ports.json, and File.chmod!(dir, 0o700) for the state dir; additionally warn or refuse when the Replay adapter would copy cassettes into a world-readable or repo-internal --dir, mirroring the recorder's check_out.",
              "cwe_id": "CWE-732",
              "snippet": "    File.write!(tmp, JSON.encode!(value))",
              "symbol": "KomkiMock.Server.write_json/2 (via persist/1)",
              "declared_line": 254,
              "claudeSecurityPluginFindingId": "v3:be5dacd4daf9417a84a95c2c3cdfaf5cc5bc95673172d3dfd545e5f323b99c5a",
              "verification": {
                "panel": {
                  "true": 3,
                  "false": 0,
                  "voters": 3
                }
              }
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-203",
          "ruleIndex": 5,
          "level": "warning",
          "message": {
            "text": "vault_search existence oracle: Γ-membership redaction runs before the silent B_W filter, so hidden notes poison the whole answer instead of being dropped.\n\nThe authorized but untrusted caller (the model pi) controls the vault_search text; the vault connector returns every note matching that text whose front-matter labels are merely binding-shaped (shape-only check in KomkiConnectorVault.label?/1), including labels not present in Γ. In ConnectorGate.admit/5 the Γ-membership redaction (labels/4 → note_labels_ok? → `{:redact, ..., [\"label_not_in_gamma\"]}`) is evaluated on the UNFILTERED hit list at line 845, before the documented silent B_W filter visible/3 at line 846 can drop such hits — producing exactly the distinguishable outcomes (\"permit, N hits\" vs \"redacted, label_not_in_gamma\") that the filter's own comment says must not exist.\n\nImpact: The agent learns that a note invisible to it (labels outside Γ) exists and matches a chosen substring; since the connector matches by String.contains? over title and body (query up to 200 chars), adaptive queries let the model reconstruct fragments of hidden notes' content character by character. Additionally, when a hidden note matches, every legitimately admissible hit for that query is withheld (the whole answer is redacted), a content-dependent availability failure of vault_search.\n\nExploit scenario: The user keeps a private note with front matter `komki_labels: [privat@1]` — hand-written or left over after a binding rename, since bindings are immutable (R.2.1) and Γ is the label-store export. pi calls vault_search {\"text\": \"geheim\"}; instead of the silent \"0 hits\" the design demands, it receives {\"admitted\": \"redacted\", \"reason\": \"Labels der Notiz ausserhalb von Γ\", \"verify\": [\"label_not_in_gamma\"]}, confirming a hidden note contains \"geheim\". Iterating refined substrings reconstructs that note's content piece by piece, while each such query also suppresses all public hits.\n\nPreconditions: A vault note whose front-matter komki_labels are binding-shaped but not registered in Γ (registry.json bindings) — plausible because labels are free-form user-authored front matter and bindings are immutable, so renames orphan old labels; Caller holds the socket bearer token (the agent pi or any token-bearing local client).\n\nRecommendation: Filter before validating: for vault search, run visible/3 (or an equivalent Γ-membership drop) first and silently discard hits whose labels are not in Γ, mirroring the connector's own visibility rule — then Γ-check only the surviving hits. Keep the hard Γ redaction for single reads (vault_read), where a distinguishable deny/redact outcome is the documented, accepted tradeoff (ticket #211).\n\nCWE-203. Severity MEDIUM, confidence medium."
          },
          "locations": [
