        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-1333",
          "ruleIndex": 3,
          "level": "warning",
          "message": {
            "text": "Remote-controlled Brave result text hits quadratic-backtracking tag-strip regex before any size bound (ReDoS).\n\nBrave search-response titles/descriptions — content derived from arbitrary third-party indexed web pages, bounded only by the 2 MiB body cap at http.ex:62 — reach String.replace(text, ~r/<[^>]*>/, \"\") untruncated, because clean/2 truncates to 300/1000 chars only as its last step. On input with many '<' and no '>' the greedy [^>]* consumes to end-of-string from every '<' start and PCRE's bumpalong restarts at each, giving Θ(n²) engine work per result item; auto-possessification of [^>]* before the excluded '>' keeps counted match() calls near ~2n, below PCRE2's 10M match limit for n up to 2 MiB, so the full quadratic scan runs unthrottled.\n\nImpact: Availability only: one connector-web serve process per search is wedged at 100% CPU inside the regex engine — roughly 10^10 engine steps for a 100 KiB title and up to ~4x10^12 near the 2 MiB cap (minutes to hours per call) — while the caller has already timed out (Sidecar.Client default 15 s); each further search returning the malicious result stacks another wedged process, progressively saturating the connector's CPU. Integrity and confidentiality are unaffected.\n\nExploit scenario: An attacker publishes a page whose <title> or meta description is a long run of '<' and gets it into Brave's index. Whenever the agent's web_search query surfaces that result, Brave returns the hostile string over the (correctly TLS-verified) channel; the connector's serving process enters the quadratic scan and pegs a core long after the mediator gave up. Repeated searches wedge additional processes until the connector is effectively unusable.\n\nPreconditions: web connector running (default komki setup; no privileges or direct network reach needed by the attacker); a web_search must return the attacker-influenced result — victim/agent interaction via the query; the hostile title/description must survive Brave's own server-side limits; the component imposes no size bound before the regex (residual uncertainty, as is the exact match-limit accounting of OTP 29's PCRE2 for single-character repeats — the worst case is a raised error collapsed to an 'internal' reply instead of a wedge); brave.ex:70 snippet path: description is fed through the same clean/2, so either field suffices.\n\nRecommendation: Bound the input before any regex work: make the first step of clean/2 a hard cut of the raw string (e.g. byte_size guard ~8 KiB, or String.slice(text, 0, 4 * max) before strip_tags), and/or replace the regex with a linear scan (split on \">\", then drop each segment up to its last \"<\"). A possessive quantifier alone does not fix it: bumpalong still rescans every '<' position. The same guard should wrap the decode_entities and \\s+ passes, which are linear today but still run on the untruncated string.\n\nCWE-1333. Severity MEDIUM, confidence medium."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "apps/komki_connector_web/lib/komki_connector_web/text.ex",
                  "uriBaseId": "%SRCROOT%"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 48,
                  "snippet": {
                    "text": "defp strip_tags(text), do: String.replace(text, ~r/<[^>]*>/, \"\")"
                  }
                }
              },
              "logicalLocations": [
                {
                  "name": "strip_tags/1 (via Text.clean/2)",
                  "fullyQualifiedName": "strip_tags/1 (via Text.clean/2)"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "properties": {
            "claudeSecurityPlugin": {
