              "file": "apps/komki_connector_ris/lib/komki_connector_ris/top.ex",
              "line": 84,
              "description": "Untrusted source: the RIS HTTP response — every textblocks.content of guestagendaitems (and the raw content string when Base.decode64 fails, top.ex:76-81) — reaches the sink `String.replace(~r/<[^>]*>/, \" \")`, a backtracking-engine regex whose per-start cost is proportional to the remaining run of non-'>' bytes (O(n^2) on e.g. 1 MB of '<'). Neither the content size nor the number of textblocks is bounded anywhere, and the connector's 5 s/12 s budgets (api.ex:79) guard only the HTTP request, never the text processing that follows.",
              "exploit_scenario": "A hostile or compromised RIS (or a third party who can plant content in a public agenda item) serves guestagendaitems with many textblocks whose content is a huge run of '<' characters (or any invalid-base64 data, which falls to the raw-HTML branch). ris_show, or ris_search's automatic phase-2 text fetch, feeds each block to the regex; a single response with ~1000 such blocks (deliverable within the 5 s request timeout by a datacenter peer) keeps the serving process busy for minutes, long after the mediator answered upstream-timeout. With no match limit in force, a single 1 MB block alone would be quadratic (order hours).",
