        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-400",
          "ruleIndex": 8,
          "level": "warning",
          "message": {
            "text": "Unbounded in-process PDF render: an admitted document can OOM or crash the entire connector VM.\n\nUntrusted room-document bytes (PDFs supplied by outside parties at intake) are rendered entirely inside the one documents connector VM: `ExPdfium.open` (pdf.ex:72) reads up to 64 MiB into the BEAM and parses it natively in-process, `add_page/3` materializes each page's complete text before the 1 MiB accumulation check at line 139 ever runs, and `with_marked_text/3` (pdf.ex:187) turns every glyph of a page into a BEAM map with no bound at all — the code itself concedes \"no memory limit in the profile\" (komki_connector_documents.ex:30-32). A decompression-bomb page (a small Flate stream expanding to gigabytes of text-showing operators, confirmed unbounded in the vendored NIF: `page.text().all()` builds the whole page string, `document_text_chars` builds one struct per glyph) forces multi-gigabyte allocations in the serving VM, and a native parser fault cannot be caught by the `rescue` at pdf.ex:63-69, which only sees Elixir exceptions — either way the single documents connector of the instance dies with all in-flight requests.\n\nImpact: Availability of the documents connector for the whole komki instance: one hostile admitted PDF crashes the VM or gets it OOM-killed, every `admit_document` call of every session then fails with upstream-error until someone notices and re-runs `komki up`; because the sandbox profile sets no memory limit, the resource pressure is unbounded and can degrade the whole machine. No confidentiality or integrity impact (content stays inside the designed Gate flow).\n\nExploit scenario: An external actor sends a council member a crafted ~10 MB PDF whose page 1 is a Flate stream expanding to gigabytes of text-drawing operators. The member intakes it into a workspace (intake passes: `%PDF-` header present, sha-registered, labels permitting admission — intake is the designed permit). When the agent (or the member via pi) calls `admit_document` on it, `ExPdfium.extract_text` on the bomb page allocates the decompressed page text in native memory plus a BEAM copy (and `ExPdfium.chars` a map per glyph if a highlight sits on that page) until the connector VM is killed — taking down document admission for all rooms of the instance.\n\nPreconditions: The victim must intake the attacker's PDF as a room document and it must carry labels the Gate admits — the attacker cannot push it in remotely.; admit_document must actually be called on that document_id; the attacker can only influence the choice via an enticing filename/first page.; Exploitation relies on a crafted PDF expanding far beyond its 64 MiB on-disk size or faulting pdfium natively — I verified the missing bounds in code but could not execute a PoC (read-only scan), hence MEDIUM confidence.\n\nRecommendation: Do not render untrusted PDFs inside the serving VM. Run the pdfium render in a separate OS process (a spawned helper under the same Seatbelt profile) with an rlimit on address space and a wall-clock timeout, and treat crash/OOM/timeout as a per-request `upstream-error` instead of losing the whole connector. Additionally, extend ex_pdfium with a bounded extraction (per-page text/char budget reported incrementally, or reject pages whose content stream decompresses beyond a fixed budget) so the 1 MiB frame limit is enforced before a page's text is fully materialized, not after (pdf.ex:139).\n\nCWE-400. Severity MEDIUM, confidence medium."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "apps/komki_connector_documents/lib/komki_connector_documents/pdf.ex",
                  "uriBaseId": "%SRCROOT%"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 134,
                  "snippet": {
                    "text": "      case ExPdfium.extract_text(doc, index) do"
                  }
                }
              },
              "logicalLocations": [
                {
                  "name": "add_page/3",
                  "fullyQualifiedName": "add_page/3"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "properties": {
            "claudeSecurityPlugin": {
              "id": "F17",
              "title": "Unbounded in-process PDF render: an admitted document can OOM or crash the entire connector VM",
              "impact": "Availability of the documents connector for the whole komki instance: one hostile admitted PDF crashes the VM or gets it OOM-killed, every `admit_document` call of every session then fails with upstream-error until someone notices and re-runs `komki up`; because the sandbox profile sets no memory limit, the resource pressure is unbounded and can degrade the whole machine. No confidentiality or integrity impact (content stays inside the designed Gate flow).",
              "file": "apps/komki_connector_documents/lib/komki_connector_documents/pdf.ex",
              "line": 134,
              "description": "Untrusted room-document bytes (PDFs supplied by outside parties at intake) are rendered entirely inside the one documents connector VM: `ExPdfium.open` (pdf.ex:72) reads up to 64 MiB into the BEAM and parses it natively in-process, `add_page/3` materializes each page's complete text before the 1 MiB accumulation check at line 139 ever runs, and `with_marked_text/3` (pdf.ex:187) turns every glyph of a page into a BEAM map with no bound at all — the code itself concedes \"no memory limit in the profile\" (komki_connector_documents.ex:30-32). A decompression-bomb page (a small Flate stream expanding to gigabytes of text-showing operators, confirmed unbounded in the vendored NIF: `page.text().all()` builds the whole page string, `document_text_chars` builds one struct per glyph) forces multi-gigabyte allocations in the serving VM, and a native parser fault cannot be caught by the `rescue` at pdf.ex:63-69, which only sees Elixir exceptions — either way the single documents connector of the instance dies with all in-flight requests.",
              "exploit_scenario": "An external actor sends a council member a crafted ~10 MB PDF whose page 1 is a Flate stream expanding to gigabytes of text-drawing operators. The member intakes it into a workspace (intake passes: `%PDF-` header present, sha-registered, labels permitting admission — intake is the designed permit). When the agent (or the member via pi) calls `admit_document` on it, `ExPdfium.extract_text` on the bomb page allocates the decompressed page text in native memory plus a BEAM copy (and `ExPdfium.chars` a map per glyph if a highlight sits on that page) until the connector VM is killed — taking down document admission for all rooms of the instance.",
              "preconditions": [
                "The victim must intake the attacker's PDF as a room document and it must carry labels the Gate admits — the attacker cannot push it in remotely.",
                "admit_document must actually be called on that document_id; the attacker can only influence the choice via an enticing filename/first page.",
                "Exploitation relies on a crafted PDF expanding far beyond its 64 MiB on-disk size or faulting pdfium natively — I verified the missing bounds in code but could not execute a PoC (read-only scan), hence MEDIUM confidence."
              ],
              "category": "Uncontrolled Resource Consumption",
              "severity": "MEDIUM",
              "confidence": "medium",
              "recommendation": "Do not render untrusted PDFs inside the serving VM. Run the pdfium render in a separate OS process (a spawned helper under the same Seatbelt profile) with an rlimit on address space and a wall-clock timeout, and treat crash/OOM/timeout as a per-request `upstream-error` instead of losing the whole connector. Additionally, extend ex_pdfium with a bounded extraction (per-page text/char budget reported incrementally, or reject pages whose content stream decompresses beyond a fixed budget) so the 1 MiB frame limit is enforced before a page's text is fully materialized, not after (pdf.ex:139).",
              "cwe_id": "CWE-400",
              "snippet": "      case ExPdfium.extract_text(doc, index) do",
              "symbol": "add_page/3",
              "declared_line": 134,
              "claudeSecurityPluginFindingId": "v3:995a94fa326b918221fa383b383a6b642c754087c9d5c52afbacbc51faf256ec",
              "verification": {
                "panel": {
                  "true": 3,
                  "false": 0,
                  "voters": 3
                }
              }
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-1021",
          "ruleIndex": 9,
          "level": "warning",
          "message": {
            "text": "The Touch ID prompt guarding every key agreement displays caller-chosen, unauthenticated reason text.\n\nThe consent dialog is the only barrier between a same-user process and the shared secret for any real envelope — whose key ref and ephemeral point are not secret (keychain items and the 0600 seal file are readable by every same-user process, secrets.ex:9-12) — but its localizedReason is the `--reason` argv of whichever process spawns the helper (main.swift:92-97; passed through core.ex:145, ratsprojekte/tresor.ex:70, ris_cassettes.exs:249). The OS shows the same binary (komki-tresor) regardless of who spawned it, so an attacker can present a prompt indistinguishable from komki's and receives the shared secret on approval.\n\nImpact: One approval of a prompt the person cannot tell apart from komki's releases the decryption of any sealed secret — the hub's SQLCipher key, the cassette master key, RIS credentials, the Brave API key — to the requesting process, which is exactly the adversary class the sealed keychain is built against.\n\nExploit scenario: Malware reads a ktv2 envelope from the keychain (same-user readable), spawns `komki-tresor agree --reason \"komki startet den Connector ris: Ratsinfo-Zugang\"` with that ref and eph on stdin; the person sees the familiar Touch ID dialog, approves, and the helper writes the shared secret to the malware's pipe; the malware runs the same HKDF-SHA256/AES-256-GCM open and holds the plaintext secret.\n\nPreconditions: Malicious code already running as the same OS user; The person approves the Touch ID prompt the attacker triggered.\n\nRecommendation: Treat the reason as untrusted display text and stop presenting it as the requester's identity (secrets.ex:193 calls it \"wer fragt und wofuer\"); if the consent must resist same-user processes, gate `agree` on something the attacker cannot supply — for example verify the spawning parent's code-signing identity against a pin established at setup, since the OS displays the same binary name in both cases.\n\nCWE-451. Severity MEDIUM, confidence medium."
          },
