        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-863",
          "ruleIndex": 1,
          "level": "warning",
          "message": {
            "text": "Secret-entry commands execute the komki-tresor key-custodian binary without the program pin, letting a planted binary capture newly sealed secrets.\n\nOn `komki secret set`, `komki secret erzeugen` and the unseal-migration in `komki setup`, the value typed by the user is sealed by executing whatever file `Tresor.binary()` resolves to (beside-komki dir, then PATH via System.find_executable, tresor.ex:44-49) with no comparison against the recorded `cfg.pins[\"komki-tresor\"]` hash; the executed program's returned public key becomes the seal recipient, and sealing performs no Touch ID prompt — so any same-uid process that plants the binary silently receives the secret's confidentiality.\n\nImpact: Confidentiality loss of every secret entered through these commands: the Brave Search API key and — via `komki secret erzeugen ratsprojekte-hub` — the freshly generated ratsprojekte hub token, a capability token authorizing writes into the political documentation hub. The seal architecture's core property (secrets bound to this Mac's Secure Enclave, resistant to same-uid keychain readers per secrets.ex moduledoc) is silently defeated for newly entered values; the tamper surfaces only later as a doctor pin FAIL (Siegel unopenable), after the secret is already stolen.\n\nExploit scenario: A process running as the user (e.g. a compromised pnpm/brew package postinstall — the toolchain komki documents installs into user-writable dirs) writes an executable `komki-tresor` into a PATH directory that wins resolution (or beside the komki binary, e.g. ~/.local/bin per the `make komki` layout). The next `komki secret set brave` resolves and executes the fake; it prints `ref public` for an attacker-generated P-256 key; komki AES-GCM-seals the entered API key to that key and stores the seal in the keychain. No Touch ID prompt occurs because sealing needs only the public key. The attacker reads the same-uid-readable keychain entry and decrypts with their private key.\n\nPreconditions: An attacker-controlled process running as the user's uid can write to the komki-tresor discovery location (directory beside the komki binary, or a PATH entry that wins resolution) — user-writable in the documented Homebrew/pnpm/~/.local/bin setups; no genuine tresor beside komki, otherwise the beside-copy wins; The user runs a secret-entry command (`komki secret set`, `komki secret erzeugen`, or `komki setup`'s seal/entry step) after the plant.\n\nRecommendation: Verify the recorded pin before executing komki-tresor on every secret-touching path: in `KomkiCli.Tresor.Core.run/4` (or `KomkiCli.Tresor.binary/0`) compare `Seatbelt.Pins.hash(command)` against `cfg.pins[\"komki-tresor\"][\"sha256\"]` and refuse on mismatch or missing pin, mirroring the `up` gate (steps.ex:631); alternatively run doctor_quick inside `komki secret set`/`secret erzeugen` before the hidden prompt.\n\nCWE-863. Severity MEDIUM, confidence medium."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "apps/komki_cli/lib/komki_cli/tresor/core.ex",
                  "uriBaseId": "%SRCROOT%"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 183,
                  "snippet": {
                    "text": "Port.open({:spawn_executable, command}, [:binary, :exit_status, :use_stdio, args: args])"
                  }
                }
              },
              "logicalLocations": [
                {
                  "name": "KomkiCli.Tresor.Core.spawn/4",
                  "fullyQualifiedName": "KomkiCli.Tresor.Core.spawn/4"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "properties": {
            "claudeSecurityPlugin": {
