        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-863",
          "ruleIndex": 1,
          "level": "warning",
          "message": {
            "text": "Verwaltung socket issues the full-session one-time link to any same-UID process behind an unattributable Touch ID prompt (confused deputy).\n\nThe untrusted source is the command line any process in the user's session can write to verwaltung.sock (mode 0600 in the 0700 home — reachable by the same UID, verwaltung.ex:64); the only authorization is a Touch ID prompt whose reason is the fixed string \"ratsprojekte entsperren\" (cli.ex:37, tresor.ex:70-71) shown by komki-tresor (main.swift:55-56, LAContext.localizedReason), so the human approves on behalf of an unidentified requester, and the sink hands the one-time session link to exactly that connecting process.\n\nImpact: Complete read and write access to the hub's SQLCipher-encrypted database (projects, opinions, drafts, briefings) for an unprivileged process in the user account — the exact adversary the Touch ID/Secure-Enclave design claims to exclude. Confidentiality and integrity of all hub data; the session marker also survives until the 30-minute idle lock.\n\nExploit scenario: Malware running as the user connects to <RATSPROJEKTE_HOME>/verwaltung.sock and sends \"oeffnen\" (queued repeatedly; the accept loop serializes one prompt per request, each raising the identical dialog). The user, about to unlock anyway via the lock-page button or `ratsprojekte open`, approves the prompt they see — it is indistinguishable from their own unlock. The socket returns \"ok http://localhost:4100/zugang/<token>\" to the malware, which redeems it with a plain GET (no CSRF needed for safe methods) and receives the _ratsprojekte_key session cookie, gaining full interface access with no further biometric prompt.\n\nPreconditions: Attacker-controlled code executing as the same local user as the hub (the adversary the component's own threat model names); The person at the Mac approving one of the indistinguishable Touch ID/password prompts — realistic precisely while they are unlocking the hub themselves.\n\nRecommendation: Bind the human's approval to the requester: include a per-request nonce in the Touch ID reason passed to komki-tresor (the reason is already an argument, tresor.ex:70-71) and display the same nonce in the requesting channel (terminal prompt or lock-page dialog), or gate socket-initiated unlocks on a per-boot capability file that only the genuine CLI can read. At minimum, rate-limit outstanding oeffnen requests so a background process cannot queue a stream of prompts that blend into the user's own unlock.\n\nCWE-863. Severity MEDIUM, confidence medium."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "apps/ratsprojekte/lib/ratsprojekte/verwaltung.ex",
                  "uriBaseId": "%SRCROOT%"
