        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-522",
          "ruleIndex": 12,
          "level": "note",
          "message": {
            "text": "Recovery code (the raw SQLCipher master key) is typed with terminal echo enabled in `ratsprojekte recover`.\n\nThe recovery code is the database key itself, Base32-encoded (Schluessel.code/1, schluessel.ex:40-46; aus_code/1 decodes it back to the same 32 bytes), and the default `IO.gets/1` provider (cli.ex:108) reads it with TTY echo left on — the same repo's KomkiCli.Prompt.secret/1 (apps/komki_cli/lib/komki_cli/prompt.ex:94) implements `stty -echo` precisely because reading a key with echo is unacceptable in this CLI runtime. The key never rotates, so one observation decrypts the database and all its backups permanently.\n\nImpact: An observer of the terminal — shoulder-surfing, screen sharing or recording, saved scrollback/buffer files — obtains the master key of the hub database and can decrypt ratsprojekte.db, its pre-migration copy and every Time Machine backup (Ort home), permanently, since no key rotation exists.\n\nExploit scenario: A person restores a Mac from Time Machine and runs `ratsprojekte recover`; they retrieve the recovery code from their password manager and type it. Unlike `einrichten`, where displaying the code once is the documented design, this second display is unintended: the full 52-character master key appears on screen and stays in the terminal's scrollback buffer (or in a screen-share session), where a later reader can use it to open the SQLCipher database directly.\n\nPreconditions: The user runs `ratsprojekte recover` on a terminal and types the code; An attacker observes the terminal live, via screen sharing, or reads its saved scrollback/buffer afterwards.\n\nRecommendation: Read the recovery code with echo suppressed, mirroring KomkiCli.Prompt.secret/1: run `stty -echo` over a `:nouse_stdio` port on fd 0, restore echo afterwards, and refuse to read the code when stdin is not a tty; or accept and explicitly document the second display.\n\nCWE-522. Severity LOW, confidence high."
          },
