        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-732",
          "ruleIndex": 4,
          "level": "note",
          "message": {
            "text": "llama-server API key persisted in pi's models.json with default file permissions while every other secret file gets 0600.\n\nwrite_models/1 embeds the live llama-server API key (read from the carefully 0600-protected <base>/tmp/llama.key) into <base>/pi-home/.pi/agent/models.json and writes it with File.write!'s default creation mode, without the create-empty/chmod-0600/fill dance the same codebase applies to every other secret file (Llama.write_key! llama.ex:159-161, Channel.write_token! channel.ex:75-81, Sidecar.Token.write! token.ex:31-34). The key copy is therefore group/world-readable per the ambient umask wherever the parent directory chain is traversable by other local users.\n\nImpact: Any local user who can traverse the base directory reads the llama API key — the only gate (besides /health) on the llama-server HTTP API bound to 127.0.0.1:<port> — and can drive the pinned local model (compute theft / degradation of shared hardware). llama-server runs stateless with --no-slots, so no cross-user conversation disclosure. The default deployment (~/.local/share/komki under a 0700 macOS home) is not exposed; exposure requires a base outside the protected home.\n\nExploit scenario: On a shared Mac, komki runs an instance with KOMKI_HOME=/private/tmp/komki-test (the shorter base the project's own socket-length error message recommends, and the wegwerf/smoke pattern this repo practices). create_dirs builds pi-home and its .pi/agent subdirectories with default 0755, and every `komki up` writes models.json with the ambient-umask default (0644 under umask 022) holding the fresh llama key. A second local user lists /private/tmp, reads models.json, and can now run inference against 127.0.0.1:<port> — while the instance's actual tokens (mediator.token, connector tokens, hub hash) all remain correctly 0600/0700.\n\nPreconditions: Multi-user host with other local accounts; Instance base dir (KOMKI_HOME) located where other users can traverse (e.g. /private/tmp), not the default ~/.local/share/komki inside the 0700 home; llama-server started (key written by Llama.write_key!/write_models on every `komki up`).\n\nRecommendation: Apply the same create-empty/chmod-0600/fill pattern used by Llama.write_key!/Channel.write_token!/Sidecar.Token.write! when persisting models.json (or chmod pi_home to 0700 at creation), so the key copy carries the same protection as its source file llama.key.\n\nCWE-732. Severity LOW, confidence medium."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "apps/komki_cli/lib/komki_cli/steps.ex",
                  "uriBaseId": "%SRCROOT%"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 555,
                  "snippet": {
                    "text": "    File.write!(path, models <> \"\\n\")"
                  }
                }
              },
              "logicalLocations": [
                {
                  "name": "KomkiCli.Steps.write_models/1",
                  "fullyQualifiedName": "KomkiCli.Steps.write_models/1"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "properties": {
            "claudeSecurityPlugin": {
              "id": "F24",
