        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-276",
          "ruleIndex": 6,
          "level": "note",
          "message": {
            "text": "llama-server bearer key persisted with default 0644 permissions in pi's models.json, undoing the 0600 protection the same key gets in llama.key.\n\nThe bearer key that gates the loopback llama-server API (generated via :crypto.strong_rand_bytes and deliberately chmod 0600 in <base>/tmp/llama.key by Llama.write_key!/1, llama.ex:159-161) is copied verbatim as \"apiKey\" by write_models/1 into <pi_home>/.pi/agent/models.json using a plain File.write! with no chmod; the pi_home chain is created world-traversable (create_dirs, steps.ex:339, uses mkdir_p with default 0755 and chmods only \"denials\" to 0700), so under the default umask 022 any other local account can read the key.\n\nImpact: Confidentiality loss of the llama-server API key to other local accounts. An attacker account can use it to call the loopback-only inference API (/v1/...) directly, bypassing the key-based protection introduced for exactly this class of local abuser (Ticket #136), and run inference on the victim's model and hardware without appearing in any pi session. No integrity impact; no remote exposure (the API binds 127.0.0.1 and the file is only locally reachable).\n\nExploit scenario: On a shared macOS machine, account B traverses /Users/A/.local/share/komki/pi-home/.pi/agent/models.json (all parent directories 0755, the file 0644 because komki never sets a restrictive mode and the default umask is 022), extracts the 43-character apiKey, and starts issuing chat-completion requests against http://127.0.0.1:<port>/v1 — llama-server's only defense accepts it, and B consumes A's model and compute invisibly.\n\nPreconditions: More than one local account on the host (a same-uid process can already read the 0600 llama.key, so only cross-user exposure is new); Default umask 022 when `komki up` runs (macOS default; komki never enforces a restrictive mode itself) and 0755 home/base directories; llama-server started at least once via `komki up`, which writes both llama.key and models.json on every start.\n\nRecommendation: Apply the same discipline already used for llama.key, the mediator token (channel.ex:79-81) and the sidecar tokens (sidecar/token.ex:31-34) in write_models/1: create the file empty, File.chmod!(path, 0o600), then write the content — and/or chmod the pi_home chain to 0700 in create_dirs, which also protects the session transcripts stored under pi-home.\n\nCWE-276. Severity LOW, confidence medium."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "apps/komki_cli/lib/komki_cli/steps.ex",
                  "uriBaseId": "%SRCROOT%"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 555,
                  "snippet": {
                    "text": "    File.write!(path, models <> \"\\n\")"
                  }
                }
              },
              "logicalLocations": [
                {
                  "name": "KomkiCli.Steps.write_models/1",
                  "fullyQualifiedName": "KomkiCli.Steps.write_models/1"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "properties": {
            "claudeSecurityPlugin": {
              "id": "F25",
              "title": "llama-server bearer key persisted with default 0644 permissions in pi's models.json, undoing the 0600 protection the same key gets in llama.key",
              "impact": "Confidentiality loss of the llama-server API key to other local accounts. An attacker account can use it to call the loopback-only inference API (/v1/...) directly, bypassing the key-based protection introduced for exactly this class of local abuser (Ticket #136), and run inference on the victim's model and hardware without appearing in any pi session. No integrity impact; no remote exposure (the API binds 127.0.0.1 and the file is only locally reachable).",
              "file": "apps/komki_cli/lib/komki_cli/steps.ex",
              "line": 555,
              "description": "The bearer key that gates the loopback llama-server API (generated via :crypto.strong_rand_bytes and deliberately chmod 0600 in <base>/tmp/llama.key by Llama.write_key!/1, llama.ex:159-161) is copied verbatim as \"apiKey\" by write_models/1 into <pi_home>/.pi/agent/models.json using a plain File.write! with no chmod; the pi_home chain is created world-traversable (create_dirs, steps.ex:339, uses mkdir_p with default 0755 and chmods only \"denials\" to 0700), so under the default umask 022 any other local account can read the key.",
              "exploit_scenario": "On a shared macOS machine, account B traverses /Users/A/.local/share/komki/pi-home/.pi/agent/models.json (all parent directories 0755, the file 0644 because komki never sets a restrictive mode and the default umask is 022), extracts the 43-character apiKey, and starts issuing chat-completion requests against http://127.0.0.1:<port>/v1 — llama-server's only defense accepts it, and B consumes A's model and compute invisibly.",
              "preconditions": [
                "More than one local account on the host (a same-uid process can already read the 0600 llama.key, so only cross-user exposure is new)",
                "Default umask 022 when `komki up` runs (macOS default; komki never enforces a restrictive mode itself) and 0755 home/base directories",
                "llama-server started at least once via `komki up`, which writes both llama.key and models.json on every start"
              ],
              "category": "Incorrect Default Permissions",
