        {
          "ruleId": "uncategorized",
          "ruleIndex": 13,
          "level": "note",
          "message": {
            "text": "Terminal control-sequence injection via unneutralized Sandbox-denial targets in the capture footer.\n\nSemi-untrusted `/usr/bin/log show` output — whose Sandbox-denial targets are byte strings the confined pi process chooses (file paths under verbatim prefixes like /opt or /Applications, or non-path targets such as mach-lookup names) — is parsed and path-redacted but never stripped of terminal control characters; footer/1 then emits those bytes to the user's terminal (IO.write at cli.ex:993), bypassing the Terminal.sanitize/1 standard this codebase itself enforces for pi output to prevent exactly this (OSC 52 clipboard writes, OSC 8 links, CSI line rewrites — Ticket #132).\n\nImpact: A prompt-injected agent inside the Seatbelt boundary can write arbitrary escape sequences into the komki window: CSI sequences spoof or overwrite komki's own verdict/footer lines, and OSC 52 overwrites the user's clipboard (paste-jacking when pasted into other applications). Bounded to the terminal display and clipboard; no host code execution and no sandbox escape.\n\nExploit scenario: The confined agent repeatedly attempts to open a crafted path such as /opt/x\\u001b]52;c;<base64>\\u001b\\\\ (or a mach-lookup of a service name embedding the same bytes) — each attempt is denied by Sandbox and logged by the kernel with the attacker-chosen bytes as the denial target. When the user runs `komki run --capture-denials`, parse/1 captures the raw target, redact/3 leaves it verbatim (verbatim prefix or non-path target), and the footer prints it to the user's terminal, where a supporting terminal executes the clipboard-write or line-rewrite sequence.\n\nPreconditions: User runs komki run/session with the opt-in --capture-denials flag; The crafted denial ranks in the footer's top 5 by occurrences (attacker inflates occurrences by repeating the denied operation); macOS `log show --style compact` emits the control bytes verbatim into its eventMessage output (plausible from known unified-log behavior, but not verifiable by execution in this read-only review); The user's terminal interprets the injected sequence (OSC 52 write support or CSI handling).\n\nRecommendation: Route every string built from log-derived data through KomkiCli.Terminal.sanitize/1 (or an equivalent C0/C1 escape strip) before it reaches IO.write — in footer/1 for both the denial lines and the error footer — mirroring the pi-output path; alternatively strip control characters already in parse/redact so no raw log byte survives to any display layer.\n\nCWE-150. Severity LOW, confidence medium."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "apps/komki_cli/lib/komki_cli/denials.ex",
                  "uriBaseId": "%SRCROOT%"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 593,
                  "snippet": {
                    "text": "        \"  \" <> denial.operation <> \" \" <> denial.target <> \" ×\" <> to_string(denial.occurrences)"
                  }
                }
              },
              "logicalLocations": [
                {
                  "name": "KomkiCli.Denials.footer/1",
                  "fullyQualifiedName": "KomkiCli.Denials.footer/1"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "properties": {
            "claudeSecurityPlugin": {
              "id": "F26",
              "title": "Terminal control-sequence injection via unneutralized Sandbox-denial targets in the capture footer",
              "impact": "A prompt-injected agent inside the Seatbelt boundary can write arbitrary escape sequences into the komki window: CSI sequences spoof or overwrite komki's own verdict/footer lines, and OSC 52 overwrites the user's clipboard (paste-jacking when pasted into other applications). Bounded to the terminal display and clipboard; no host code execution and no sandbox escape.",
              "file": "apps/komki_cli/lib/komki_cli/denials.ex",
              "line": 593,
              "description": "Semi-untrusted `/usr/bin/log show` output — whose Sandbox-denial targets are byte strings the confined pi process chooses (file paths under verbatim prefixes like /opt or /Applications, or non-path targets such as mach-lookup names) — is parsed and path-redacted but never stripped of terminal control characters; footer/1 then emits those bytes to the user's terminal (IO.write at cli.ex:993), bypassing the Terminal.sanitize/1 standard this codebase itself enforces for pi output to prevent exactly this (OSC 52 clipboard writes, OSC 8 links, CSI line rewrites — Ticket #132).",
              "exploit_scenario": "The confined agent repeatedly attempts to open a crafted path such as /opt/x\\u001b]52;c;<base64>\\u001b\\\\ (or a mach-lookup of a service name embedding the same bytes) — each attempt is denied by Sandbox and logged by the kernel with the attacker-chosen bytes as the denial target. When the user runs `komki run --capture-denials`, parse/1 captures the raw target, redact/3 leaves it verbatim (verbatim prefix or non-path target), and the footer prints it to the user's terminal, where a supporting terminal executes the clipboard-write or line-rewrite sequence.",
              "preconditions": [
                "User runs komki run/session with the opt-in --capture-denials flag",
                "The crafted denial ranks in the footer's top 5 by occurrences (attacker inflates occurrences by repeating the denied operation)",
                "macOS `log show --style compact` emits the control bytes verbatim into its eventMessage output (plausible from known unified-log behavior, but not verifiable by execution in this read-only review)",
                "The user's terminal interprets the injected sequence (OSC 52 write support or CSI handling)"
              ],
              "category": "Uncategorized",
              "severity": "LOW",
              "confidence": "medium",
              "recommendation": "Route every string built from log-derived data through KomkiCli.Terminal.sanitize/1 (or an equivalent C0/C1 escape strip) before it reaches IO.write — in footer/1 for both the denial lines and the error footer — mirroring the pi-output path; alternatively strip control characters already in parse/redact so no raw log byte survives to any display layer.",
              "cwe_id": "CWE-150",
              "snippet": "        \"  \" <> denial.operation <> \" \" <> denial.target <> \" ×\" <> to_string(denial.occurrences)",
              "symbol": "KomkiCli.Denials.footer/1",
              "declared_line": 593,
              "claudeSecurityPluginFindingId": "v3:df780ab72f25d0e6b2675d17e8df7aae0a04fc286d0c73cffb960d6377856353",
              "verification": {
                "panel": {
                  "true": 3,
                  "false": 0,
                  "voters": 3
                }
              }
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-345",
          "ruleIndex": 14,
          "level": "note",
          "message": {
            "text": "Unauthenticated X-Komki-Session header is recorded as session identity in the append-only PDR audit journal.\n\nThe bearer token authenticates only the mediator channel, not the session: guard_caller accepts any syntactically valid X-Komki-Session value and Env.for_call threads it unverified into every hash-chained PDR record as session_id, so any token holder — above all the pi agent, which the system itself treats as untrusted — can forge session attribution in the audit trail.\n\nImpact: Audit misattribution only: the model can journal its calls (including GO-released effects) under another session's id or a fabricated one, weakening after-the-fact accountability of the PDR chain. No authorization decision reads the value (verified: session_id flows exclusively into PDR records), and content, decisions and hash chain are unaffected.\n\nExploit scenario: A prompt-injected model runs `sh -c 'echo $KOMKI_MEDIATOR_TOKEN'` (the token is placed in pi's environment by komki_cli/channel.ex:114), then posts its own JSON-RPC tools/call to $KOMKI_MEDIATOR_SOCKET/mcp with X-Komki-Session: ses-<fabricated hex>. The mediator's gates still decide the call, but the PDR records the fabricated session id, so a later journal review attributes the call to the wrong session.\n\nPreconditions: pi sandbox profile allows network-outbound to the mediator socket (apps/komki_cli/lib/komki_cli/profile.ex:444-446) and exec of /bin/sh and node (profile.ex:491-493); KOMKI_MEDIATOR_TOKEN present in pi's environment (apps/komki_cli/lib/komki_cli/channel.ex:114); concurrent or successive pi sessions share one mediator token (one token per cold start, kept across restarts, channel.ex:8-11).\n\nRecommendation: Either stop recording the header as an identity claim (mark PDR session_id as caller-asserted and exclude it from accountability statements), or authenticate the session: give each pi start its own mediator credential (per-session token file) instead of one shared instance token, so the journal can attribute calls to the session that actually holds the credential.\n\nCWE-345. Severity LOW, confidence medium."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "apps/komki_mediator/lib/komki_mediator/http.ex",
                  "uriBaseId": "%SRCROOT%"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 128,
                  "snippet": {
                    "text": "do: assign(conn, :komki_session, session),"
                  }
                }
              },
              "logicalLocations": [
                {
                  "name": "KomkiMediator.HTTP.assign_session/1",
                  "fullyQualifiedName": "KomkiMediator.HTTP.assign_session/1"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
