        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-319",
          "ruleIndex": 15,
          "level": "note",
          "message": {
            "text": "Real Brave API key is transmitted in cleartext to http:// base URLs — including the loopback mock port any local process can bind.\n\nThe production BRAVE_API_KEY — unsealed from the macOS keychain by `komki up` even in mock mode (`credentials_line` fetches all manifest credentials regardless of upstream mode, komki_cli/connectors.ex:393) and handed to the connector on stdin — is attached to every outbound request at brave.ex:36 with no check of the transport; when the effective base URL is `http://` (web-mock mode configures `http://127.0.0.1:<port>/`, and CLI.parse_base_url/1 accepts any http host), the secret crosses the network unencrypted.\n\nImpact: Disclosure of the paid Brave Search API credential — exactly the secret the Secure-Enclave seal (Touch ID), stdin-only provisioning, argv/env/log/inspect discipline and TLS setup exist to protect — to any local process. The attacker can then use the victim's Brave subscription for their own queries (billing abuse). This crosses the design's own trust boundary: komki seals the key precisely because local processes are considered adversaries (Keychain.store even feeds `security -i` via stdin to keep it out of `ps`).\n\nExploit scenario: A malicious local process (any uid; binding a free loopback TCP port > 1024 needs no privileges) binds 127.0.0.1:<web_mock_port> on a machine where komki runs in web-mock mode while the legitimate mock is absent or between restarts. The user runs `komki up` — approving the Touch ID prompt that unseals the real key into the connector — and any `web_search` call makes the connector send `X-Subscription-Token: <real key>` in cleartext to the attacker's listener.\n\nPreconditions: Web mock mode configured (explicit `komki init --web-mock-port N`, non-default): launcher passes `--base-url http://127.0.0.1:<port>/` and omits `--proxy`; A local process (same or different uid) holds/wins the bind on 127.0.0.1:<port> instead of the legitimate mock listener; Victim runs `komki up` (unsealing the real key) and performs at least one `web_search`; Aggravator: CLI.parse_base_url/1 also accepts `http://` for non-loopback hosts, so any operator-passed cleartext base URL exposes the key the same way.\n\nRecommendation: Attach the subscription token only on encrypted transports: in `Connector.search/3`/`Brave.headers/1` check the effective URL scheme (and host) and refuse to send the real key over `http://` — or gate cleartext to an explicit mock flag under which the launcher (`credentials_line`) provisions a synthetic test credential instead of unsealing the keychain one. At minimum, restrict `CLI.parse_base_url/1` to https except for loopback hosts.\n\nCWE-319. Severity LOW, confidence medium."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "apps/komki_connector_web/lib/komki_connector_web/brave.ex",
                  "uriBaseId": "%SRCROOT%"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 36,
                  "snippet": {
                    "text": "{~c\"x-subscription-token\", String.to_charlist(api_key)}"
                  }
                }
              },
              "logicalLocations": [
                {
                  "name": "KomkiConnectorWeb.Brave.headers/1",
                  "fullyQualifiedName": "KomkiConnectorWeb.Brave.headers/1"
                }
              ]
            }
          ],
          "properties": {
            "claudeSecurityPlugin": {
              "id": "F28",
